coronavirus first emerged in the Chinese  city of Wuhan in December there have  been tens of thousands of cases but  globally just 2% of those who have  contracted it have died  compare that with SARS which kills 10  percent and MERS  which killed 1/3 so how is it spread  it's clear the virus can be passed on  through close physical contacts and  there's growing evidence that it can be  passed on through droplets in a sneeze  or a cough some patients are reported to  develop conjunctivitis suggesting it can  be transmitted through the eyes and the  virus can survive for up to four days on  hard surfaces meaning it could be passed  on through indirect contact scientists  are monitoring how the current outbreak  develops to learn more the countries  outside of China and the cruise ships  are very important to help us better  understand what's called the  epidemiology of the disease how easily  it spreads from person to person  how fine-tuning on understanding of how  it spread and also looking at the  natural history of the disease from the  time of infection through to sickness  and either recovery or death despite the  large number of cases relatively few  people have died China says 80% of fatal  cases have been in people over 60 while  three-quarters had pre-existing  conditions but there are cases of  apparently healthy young people dying  including the Chinese doctor Lee wen  Yang who was just 34 meanwhile  scientists are still trying to work out  where the corona virus originated one  theory is that it came from bats which  passed it on to snakes which then passed  it on to humans but Chinese researchers  are now suggesting it may have come from  pangolins one of Asia's most trafficked  mammals the truth is very little is  known about this novel coronavirus
 

